{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman who ruled as the Count of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He held this position for many years and played a critical role in the politics of the Crusader states. His life was defined by complex alliances and frequent conflicts with neighboring Muslim powers as well as internal Christian factions. Raymond faced challenges from powerful rivals within the Kingdom of Jerusalem while trying to maintain stability in his own county. His strategic mind often guided the region through turbulent times when external threats loomed large.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born into a prestigious family with strong ties to the Latin East. His father was Raymond II who died after an assassination attempt by the Hashashin sect. This tragic event left the young Raymond to inherit the county at a very tender age. He required a regent to govern on his behalf until he reached maturity. The regency period involved intense political maneuvering among the barons of Tripoli. Raymond eventually took full control and began to expand his influence across the coastal regions. He sought to strengthen the defenses of his cities against potential sieges by foreign armies.\n\n*Relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\nThe Count of Tripoli had a complicated relationship with the kings of Jerusalem. He often acted as a mediator during succession crises or disputes among the high nobility. Raymond supported Baldwin III and later Amalric I despite their differences in policy. He served as a regent for the young Baldwin IV after the king contracted leprosy. This role placed him at the center of power struggles involving the royal court. Some nobles viewed him with suspicion while others relied on his experience and diplomatic skills. His influence waned when Guy of Lusignan rose to prominence and gained the favor of the queen.\n\n*Later Years and Death*\nRaymond faced increasing isolation as political tides shifted against him in the late 1180s. He clashed with Guy of Lusignan over the control of the kingdom and the response to Saladin. Raymond eventually allied with Saladin to protect his own interests from the aggressive policies of the king. This decision caused a rift between him and many other Christian leaders. He died shortly after the Battle of Hattin in 1187. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli. The loss of such an experienced leader left the region vulnerable to the advancing Muslim forces. His legacy remains significant in the history of the Crusades due to his long tenure and political acumen.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0014824525}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.003951359}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.08935969}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0037892638}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.78689563}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.031599075}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.08593307}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.101239175}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.6115856}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -2.4355516}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.00112949}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.00013960383}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0037710753}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.039401207}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.01010632}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -3.8147045e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -3.576285e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.10784742}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.46127075}, {"token": " held", "logprob": -0.77730036}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.6895079}, {"token": " position", "logprob": -0.8514014}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.21523474}, {"token": " many", "logprob": -0.23988609}, {"token": " years", "logprob": -0.34324643}, {"token